      "text": "To find the largest value of \\( c \\) such that \\(-2\\) is in the range of the function \\( f(x) = x^2 + 3x + c \\), we need to ensure that there exists some \\( x \\) for which \\( f(x) = -2 \\). \n\nFirst, we set up the equation:\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c = -2\n\\]\n\nWe can rearrange this to form a standard quadratic equation:\n\\[\nx^2 + 3x + c + 2 = 0\n\\]\n\nTo guarantee that this quadratic equation has a real solution, the discriminant must be non-negative. For a quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\), the discriminant (\\(\\Delta\\)) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\n\nIn our case, \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 3 \\), and \\( c = -2 - c \\). Substituting these values into the discrimiinate formula:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 3^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-2 - c) \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta = 9 + 8 + 4c \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nCombining like terms:\n\n\\[\n17 + 4c \\geq 0\n\\]\n\nSubtracting 17 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n4c \\geq -17\n\\]\n\nDividing by 4:\n\n\\[\nc \\geq -\\frac{17}{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the largest value of \\( c \\) that satisfies this condition is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{-\\frac{17}{4}}\n\\]",
